  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
